 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166938 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166938 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166985 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166985 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166985 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166985 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166985 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166985 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Systems of ODEs Using Variation of Parameters" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166985 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186167896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166985 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166985 #edb/ref 1050 1500.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166985 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "797cab7f-b62f-590f-9f20-4093fac9d314" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167184 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167184 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167184 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167184 #edb/ref 1069 "q-330877" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167184 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167184 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that {{field-1}} matrix.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-2}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-3}} both sides of the equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167184 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ad3fcdc-246d-580f-b053-3062ecfa179a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167184 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L4}.$ Since $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are solutions to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ i.e., by substituting any column $c(t)$ of $Φ$ into the homogeneous system, we get\n\n$$\n{c}^{′}(t)=Ac(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}^{′}(t)$ is the corresponding column of derivatives in $Φ.$ As a result, this means that ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that $Φ\\text{ is a fundamental}$ matrix.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Since $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are linearly independent. So, $Φ$ is invertible. Therefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by $\\text{multiplying by }{Φ}^{-1}$ both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\cdot Φ{u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ ({Φ}^{-1}Φ)\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ To get $\\text{L7},$ we are $\\text{integrating}$ both sides of the equation in $\\text{L6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167185 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167185 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167185 #edb/ref 1069 "q-330893" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167185 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167185 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}}},\\,Φ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-10{e}^{-2t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186167185 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a01f2ab9-e122-54f4-be8c-8d60cf41b11c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167185 #edb/ref 1074 "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2t}\\cdot 2{e}^{4t}-(-3{e}^{4t})\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{4t} & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-6t}}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{4t} & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-2t} & 3{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-2t} & 3{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -4{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}-10{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -4{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int -10{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -4{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186167896 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167896 #edb/ref 1061 592305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167896 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "94760db8-7585-5363-8838-c0f44735b806" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167896 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186167991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167897 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167897 #edb/ref 1061 592306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167897 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4a088561-5bc3-556f-b5d5-470901c2c1cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167897 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167898 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167898 #edb/ref 1061 592309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167898 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "387d38d2-d1da-59ba-b0d2-529bed76253e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167898 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167899 #edb/ref 1061 592312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167899 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9a3cf6ac-30bf-5301-9c2b-c2870c934a65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167899 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167991 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the system of ordinary differential equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) =\nA\n\\mathbf{x}(t) +\n\\mathbf{f}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix and $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is a forcing vector.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll derive the formula for the particular solution of the above system using the method of variation of parameters.\n\nRecall that the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_c(t)  = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{c}$ is a vector of constants, and $\\Phi$ is the fundamental matrix.\n\nAs usual, for the method of variation of parameters, we'll look for a particular solution of our inhomogeneous system by varying the constant parameters, i.e., in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t)  = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined.\n\nDifferentiating $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}^{′}(t) & =(Φ\\,u(t))^{′} \\\\  & =Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a solution, substituting it back into the initial system gives a true equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =Ax(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t) & =AΦu(t)+f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, since $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, $\\Phi' = A \\Phi.$ Substituting this into the equation simplifies it to the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ{u}^{′}(t)+AΦu(t) & =AΦu(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t) & =f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $\\Phi$ is invertible, we can pre-multiply both sides by $\\Phi^{-1}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\cdot Φ{u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate both sides of the final equations (ignoring the constant term):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \\Phi \\displaystyle\\int \\Phi^{-1}\\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167991 #edb/ref 1065 14396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167991 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "99630576-f78f-593a-af83-87c98421e2c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167991 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302508 #edb/ref 1080 "-18t-15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302508 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302508 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "08055b09-dc8e-5e64-a169-6b8646c97e18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302569 #edb/ref 1080 "integrating" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302569 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302569 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "112cd61c-d6ec-5b21-a43e-abee24d650f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302580 #edb/ref 1080 "differentiating" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302580 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/re